    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/lamar/jobs/5466295/director-of-archives-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Director of Archives, Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Careers at Lamar University | . For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. \n        Here are the instructions how to enable JavaScript in your web browser Director of Archives, Assistant Professor | . page has loaded. Director of Archives, Assistant Professor | . page has loaded. Click here or press any key to continue. Careers at Lamar University Menu Careers at Lamar University Sign In Don't have an account? Create one . Already have an account? Sign In . User profile dropdown menu for Username Applications Inbox Profile Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Applications Inbox Profile Go to Background Investigation Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Welcome! Thanks for creating an account. . powered by NEOGOV ® Need this page in another language? You can translate this page using your browser's built-in translation feature. Tip: In Chrome or Edge, right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Translate to [Language].\" Director of Archives, Assistant Professor Print Apply ﻿ Director of Archives, Assistant Professor Salary See Position Description Location Beaumont, TX Job Type Full-Time Faculty Job Number 202600456 Home Org Library Services Opening Date 08/31/2026 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Questions Job Summary The Director of Archives, Assistant Professor provides leadership and oversight for the University's Archives and Special Collections program. The position is responsible for the acquisition, appraisal, preservation, organization, description, digitization, and accessibility of archival collections while ensuring responsible stewardship of the University's historical records. The Director collaborates with academic departments, administrative offices, and external partners to enhance archival services, support research and instruction, and preserve the University's institutional history. Essential Job Functions Archives Administration & Program Leadership: Provide strategic leadership and day-to-day management of the University Archives and Special Collections program. Establish priorities, assess services, and implement continuous improvements to archives operations. Develop departmental goals and contribute to library strategic initiatives. Administer archives policies, procedures, and operational workflows. Prepare reports, assess program effectiveness, and participate in budget planning and stewardship. Collection Development, Preservation & Digital Archives: Direct the acquisition, appraisal, accessioning, processing, arrangement, and description of physical and digital archival collections. Oversee preservation, conservation, environmental monitoring, and long-term stewardship of archival materials. Manage digitization initiatives and digital repository workflows. Administer digital archives systems, metadata practices, and online access to collections. Lead development and maintenance of finding aids, inventories, collection guides, and descriptive tools. Collaborate with the Records Management Officer and university departments regarding transfer and preservation of official university records. Research, Instruction & Public Services: Provide reference and research assistance related to University Archives and Special Collections. Develop and deliver archival instruction sessions for faculty, students, and researchers. Support classroom instruction through archival collections and institutional history resources. Respond to research inquiries and facilitate appropriate access to archival materials. Outreach, Collaboration & Staff Leadership: Develop outreach initiatives that promote awareness and use of archival collections. Build collaborative relationships with faculty, staff, alumni, donors, community organizations, and external partners. Supervise archives personnel, student workers, interns, and project teams as assigned. Represent University Archives on library and university committees and participate in professional activities. Minimum and Desired Qualifications Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in library and information science, Library Science, Archival Studies, or a closely related field from an American Library Association (ALA)-accredited institution. Graduate-level preparation in archival management or equivalent archival training and professional experience. Minimum two years of progressively responsible experience in archives or special collections. Experience managing archival processing, preservation, description, and access for physical and digital collections. Experience organizing archival materials using recognized archival standards. Experience creating and maintaining aids and collection management tools. Experience providing archival instruction, reference, or research services. Demonstrated supervisory, organizational, project management, and communication skills. Experience working collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, and external stakeholders. Proficiency using archival and digital repository platforms such as ArchivesSpace, CONTENTdm, TARO, Springshare products, or similar systems. Working knowledge of metadata standards, archival description, digital preservation, appraisal methods, and records management principles. Ability to meet university tenure and promotion requirements, if applicable. Desired Qualifications: Undergraduate or additional graduate degree in History, Public History, Literature, or a related discipline. Experience supervising professional staff, student employees, interns, or volunteers. Experience leading exhibits, instructional programming, or public outreach initiatives. Experience managing born-digital collections and digital preservation programs. Experience with institutional repositories and digital scholarship initiatives. Academy of Certified Archivists (ACA) Certification. Supplemental Information Key Competencies: Leadership and organizational effectiveness Archives and records management Collection stewardship and preservation Digital archives and repository management Research and reference services Instruction and outreach Strategic planning and project management Written and verbal communication Collaboration and relationship building Problem solving and sound judgment Physical Requirements: Requires the ability to sit, stand, walk, bend, reach, and use a computer for extended periods. Must be able to retrieve, shelve, and transport archival materials and boxes weighing up to 20 pounds. Work may involve exposure to dust, aged paper, and archival storage environments. Job Conditions: Work is performed primarily in a library and archival environment. Responsibilities include occasional evening or weekend activities to support outreach events, instruction, or special projects. The position requires adherence to university, state, and federal regulations governing records management, archival stewardship, and public access to information. Pay Range: $65,000 - $77,000 Special Note: We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must be authorized to work in the United States without the need for sponsorship now or in the future. Finalists for this position are subject to a criminal history background check, employment history check, and off-list reference checks. Applicants are required to sign an authorization for release of prior employment records to Lamar University. We will only contact prior employers and off-list references for finalists. Who We Are: Home to more than 18,000 students, Lamar University is among the fastest growing Texas colleges and universities. LU is a member of The Texas State University System, which is the first higher education system of Texas and maintains the lowest average tuition and fees of any university system in Texas. With more than 120-degree options and a community that cares, Lamar students are always proud to be Cardinals. Lamar University strives to educate leaders, demonstrate excellence in student learning and career readiness, and pursue research with relevance. Lamar University is committed to fostering an environment of teamwork where all students, faculty, and staff can learn, work, and become a part of our community. Benefits: Health, dental & vision insurance? Life insurance State of Texas Retirement plans Tuition covered at 100% for employees, spouses and/or eligible dependents. Paid time off – vacation, sick, and holidays.? To see more information about the available benefits, please visit: Benefits - Lamar University Lamar University is An Equal Opportunity Employer Lamar University is proud to provide employment preference to veteran applicants in accordance with Texas 805 Government Code, Section 657.003. This position is security-sensitive and thereby subject to the provisions of the Texas Education Code §51.215, which authorizes the employer to obtain criminal history record information. 01 Do you have a Master’s degree in library and information science, Library Science, Archival Studies, or a closely related field from an American Library Association (ALA)-accredited institution? Yes No 02 Do you have Graduate-level preparation in archival management or equivalent archival training and professional experience? Yes No 03 Do you have a minimum two years of progressively responsible experience in archives or special collections? Yes No 04 Do you have proficiency using archival and digital repository platforms such as ArchivesSpace, CONTENTdm, TARO, Springshare products, or similar systems? Yes No 05 Do you have working knowledge of metadata standards, archival description, digital preservation, appraisal methods, and records management principles? Yes No 06 Will you now or in the future require sponsorship? Yes No Required Question Employer Lamar University Address 1060 East Virginia St. Beaumont, Texas, 77705 Phone 409-880-8375 Website https://www.lamar.edu/ Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Careers at Lamar University Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other accessibility standards. We are continually enhancing our platforms to\n                    ensure greater accessibility, and we actively seek out opportunities to improve accessibility features. Ongoing Enhancements Accessibility is an ongoing effort, and we are regularly updating our technology, services, and content\n                    to address accessibilit